WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.880
Some of the important reactions in Organic Chemistry involve generating new carbon-carbon bonds.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:15.414
Especially ones in which we add new alkyl groups to a benzene ring or some other aromatic structure.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.620
So one of these reactions is the Friedel-Crafts Alkylation.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:27.080
This is where we are able to add some alkyl fragment to a benzene ring or some other aromatic structure.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.680
So what is happening here is we have a benzene ring and we have an alkyl chloride – could be any alkyl chloride.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.260
I've drawn this one arbitrarily.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:46.700
And then in the presence of aluminum trichloride, lewis acid catalyst, we're going to get this benzene ring with the alkyl substituent and then HCl byproduct.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.740
So this actually operates in a very similar manner to the halogenation we just saw.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.000
So once again the first step is one in which we're generating a catalytic intermediate.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:10.840
So a lone pair on the chlorine atom is going to go and interact with the aluminum atom in the aluminum trichloride very similarly to the way we were seeing the iron compound behaving before.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:21.500
And so we're going to generate this compound here.  Once again this chlorine is going to have a formal positive charge, because it is contributing 6 electrons to this lewis dot structure.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:28.980
And then the aluminum atom will have a formal negative charge, because it is now contributing 4 electrons to this lewis dot structure. 

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.920
Now the only difference here from the halogenation is that there is now a further step.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.280
The benzene ring is not going to directly interact with this intermediate.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:52.060
  Instead what is going to happen is this carbon-chlorine bond is going to cleave and it's going to do so because it will then stabilize this chlorine atom and neutralize it and then we'll have an alkyl carbocation.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:02:03.360
We'll have this alkyl cation intermediate instead. So now we have our isopropyl cation and then we have the remainder of the catalytic complex. 

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:12.280
The aluminum will still have a formal negative charge there.  Aluminum has 3 valence electrons, here it is showing 4.  That means it has a formal negative charge.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:32.620
So now this is a big difference because whereas a benzene ring would have a very difficult time interacting with this neutral chloroalkane, there's going to be a much lower activation energy associated with benzene interacting with this highly unstable carbocation.

00:02:32.691 --> 00:02:39.691
The instability dictates it's going to be much more reactive and therefore able to react even with benzene.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.300
So this pi bond is going to interact with the carbocation.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.040
Now that alkyl fragment is attached to the ring just as we've seen before.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.440
Now we have a cation on the ring and then the remainder of this structure

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.860
In order to neutralize this aluminum atom, the chlorine atom leaves with the electrons in this bond

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.620
Goes extracts the proton, the electrons left behind form the new pi bond

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.980
And we've restored aromaticity.  So now we have that alkyl fragment present on the benzene ring.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.980
And then we have HCl and then we've regenerated the catalyst.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.480
So this is extremely similar to the halogenation mechanism we just saw.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.000
The only difference is that the benzene ring doesn't immediately react with this catalytic complex.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:32.120
There is yet a second step where this carbon-chlorine bond cleaves generating a carbocation intermediate and it is the carbocation intermediate that interacts initially with the benzene ring.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.440
